---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { buildBreadcrumbSchema } from "../lib/schema";

const path = "/mentoring";
const schema = buildBreadcrumbSchema([
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: "Founder mentoring", path }
]);
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Founder Mentoring | Daniel Johnson | We Scale Startups"
  description="Practical 1:1 founder mentoring with Daniel Johnson. Get clear on the customer, problem, messaging, growth strategy and next experiment, then turn it into action."
  path={path}
  schema={schema}
  breadcrumbItems={[
    { name: "Home", path: "/" },
    { name: "Founder mentoring", path }
  ]}
  headerCtaLabel="Contact me"
  headerCtaHref="/contact"
  stickyBookHref="/contact"
  stickyBookCtaLabel="Contact me"
>
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">Founder mentoring</p>
      <h1>Get clear on what matters, then do the next thing.</h1>
      <div class="hero-support">
        <p class="lede">
          I work with founders and operators who are stuck on a growth, GTM or commercial problem. We strip it back to the fundamentals, challenge the assumptions, and turn the conversation into a decision, an experiment or a clear next step.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button button-dark" href="/contact" data-cta="mentoring-contact">Contact me →</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell strategic-grid">
      <article class="strategic-panel">
        <span>01</span>
        <h2>Start with the foundations.</h2>
        <p>Before we jump into channels, tactics or growth hacks, I usually come back to four questions.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Who is the customer?</li>
          <li>What problem are we actually solving?</li>
          <li>How does the customer describe that problem?</li>
          <li>What journey do they take before becoming an active user or customer?</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="strategic-panel strategic-panel-dark">
        <span>02</span>
        <h2>Turn assumptions into evidence.</h2>
        <p>Founders often have plenty of ideas. The useful question is which ones are true. We separate what you know from what you believe, then work out the fastest way to learn.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Challenge the assumptions behind the plan</li>
          <li>Use customer conversations and real behaviour</li>
          <li>Define a small, falsifiable experiment</li>
          <li>Get a first iteration out, rather than waiting for perfect</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
      <article class="strategic-panel">
        <span>03</span>
        <h2>Leave with something to do.</h2>
        <p>I do not want mentoring to become an interesting conversation that changes nothing. The aim is to create momentum.</p>
        <ul>
          <li>A decision to make</li>
          <li>An experiment to run</li>
          <li>Homework before the next session</li>
          <li>A simple review of what you did, what is next, and what is blocking you</li>
        </ul>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">How I work</p>
          <h2>One-off clarity, or ongoing accountability.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>You can use mentoring for one difficult decision or as a regular cadence while you work through a bigger goal. Ongoing sessions are usually built around a clear objective, actions between calls, and honest feedback on what is and is not working.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>Think from first principles</h3>
          <p>We work backwards from the customer, the problem and the outcome, rather than starting with a favourite tactic or channel.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>Use the customer's language</h3>
          <p>Positioning gets stronger when it reflects how customers describe their problem, not how the company describes its product.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>Build the skill, not dependence</h3>
          <p>Mentoring is there to help you make better decisions yourself. If you need someone to do the work for you, that is consulting.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <div>
          <p class="eyebrow">Track record</p>
          <h2>Hundreds of founder conversations, grounded in operating work.</h2>
        </div>
        <p>I have supported founders through GrowthMentor, MentorCruise, Google for Startups, Techstars and other startup programmes, alongside hands-on growth work through We Scale Startups.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="grid-3">
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>479+ founder sessions</h3>
          <p>Across mentoring, accelerator and advisory conversations.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>4.97/5 average mentor rating</h3>
          <p>Independent feedback from founders I have mentored.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="feature-card">
          <h3>Operator perspective</h3>
          <p>Advice shaped by real positioning, acquisition, paid media, app growth and GTM work, not theory alone.</p>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-band">
    <div class="shell cta-inner">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Get in touch</p>
        <h2>Tell me what you are trying to work through.</h2>
        <p>If I think I can help, we can start with the problem in front of you. If what you actually need is execution rather than mentoring, I will say so.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="button button-brand" href="/contact" data-cta="mentoring-final-contact">Contact me →</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
